  "prompt_text": "The figure is a conceptual comparison of how three nutrients move from river headwaters through reservoirs to the coastal zone: nitrogen, phosphorus, and silicon. In each case, a colored arrow represents the nutrient load traveling downstream, while widening blue shapes suggest increasing water storage and burial in reservoirs.\n\nA common message is that human activities alter river chemistry before nutrients reach the coast, but they affect each element differently. For nitrogen, inputs from agriculture, wastewater, and fixation enlarge the riverine load, and the diagram emphasizes both denitrification and reservoir burial as important removal processes. Even so, anthropogenic nitrogen appears to exceed natural nitrogen in the coastal export. For phosphorus, agriculture and wastewater strongly increase inputs, but reservoirs trap a large fraction through burial, shown as a particularly large loss. For silicon, cultivation and deforestation are linked to reduced dissolved silica relative to natural conditions, with additional in-stream elimination and reservoir burial lowering export to the coast.\n\nOverall, the figure highlights reservoirs as major biogeochemical filters and shows that human disturbance tends to raise nitrogen and phosphorus delivery while decreasing silicon, shifting nutrient ratios delivered to coastal waters.",
